# Phase 18 GO_AWAY post-swap retry follow-up — 2026-10-16 16:44 UTC

## Scope
- Recheck GO_AWAY behavior against pinned RocketMQ Java 5.5.1 source and close
  the deterministic post-replacement retry discrepancy discovered during the
  Remoting P18-V01 audit.
- This is a bounded source/API/control-flow slice. Broad P18-V01 and final
  release gates remain open.

## Source and changes
- Product-code commit: `bc970e10`.
- Pinned source: Java commit `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`,
  `NettyRemotingClient.invokeImpl` and `ChannelWrapper.reconnect`.
- Java retains the old-channel-to-wrapper lookup. Once reconnect swaps the
  wrapper's current future, a later GO_AWAY response arriving on the old
  channel retries through the wrapper's current future. The wrapper uses
  `tryLock`; a callback racing before that identity swap can fail when the lock
  is held.
- Rust `retire_for_go_away` now lets requests from an already-retired
  generation reuse the shared replacement. The transport regression first
  observes the initial retry on the replacement, then sends the second GO_AWAY
  on the old channel and checks that its retry uses the same replacement.
- Updated the Phase 3 Remoting inventory and Known Differences to describe the
  post-swap path and the unclaimed `tryLock` contention ordering.

## Verification
- `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-remoting --all-features -- --test-threads=1`
  — passed: 104 unit, 8 codec, 1 fuzz regression, 7 security, 102 transport,
  and 8 doctests.
- `cargo clippy -p rocketmq-classic-remoting --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
  — passed.
- `cargo fmt --all -- --check` — passed.
- `git diff --check` — passed.
- The focused post-swap regression passed after formatting.

